WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.959
SQL injection prevention in .

2
00:00:01.959 --> 00:00:10.472
NET starts with a simple defensive idea: data supplied to an application must stay data when it reaches the database boundary.

3
00:00:10.472 --> 00:00:24.390
The risk appears when query construction allows input values, report filters, search terms, customer identifiers, order statuses, or date ranges to be interpreted as part of the database instruction itself.

4
00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:31.619
The primary prevention strategy is safe query construction, not trying to identify every suspicious string.

5
00:00:31.619 --> 00:00:42.227
Blocking a few characters or replacing text is fragile because business data changes, database syntax varies, and applications reuse values in many contexts.

6
00:00:42.227 --> 00:00:49.456
The stronger pattern is to keep command text and parameter values separate through provider-supported APIs.

7
00:00:49.456 --> 00:00:50.807
.

8
00:00:50.807 --> 00:00:53.915
NET applications reach databases in many ways.

9
00:00:53.915 --> 00:00:55.266
A team may use ADO.

10
00:00:55.266 --> 00:01:08.239
NET command objects, Entity Framework Core, Dapper or another micro-ORM, stored procedures, reporting components, background jobs, import tools, administrative utilities, or migration scripts.

11
00:01:08.239 --> 00:01:14.792
SQL injection prevention has to cover all of those paths, not only the most visible web endpoint.

12
00:01:14.792 --> 00:01:18.373
Injection prevention is part of ordinary engineering.

13
00:01:18.373 --> 00:01:21.413
Code review should notice query construction.

14
00:01:21.413 --> 00:01:24.319
Tests should cover risky data-access paths.

15
00:01:24.319 --> 00:01:28.913
Maintenance should include dependency review and legacy remediation.

16
00:01:28.913 --> 00:01:37.088
Teams should document approved patterns so new features do not reintroduce unsafe construction after the initial cleanup.

17
00:01:37.088 --> 00:01:38.439
Defense is layered.

18
00:01:38.439 --> 00:01:41.817
Parameterized access prevents the central problem.

19
00:01:41.817 --> 00:01:47.290
Least privilege reduces blast radius if an application path behaves unexpectedly.

20
00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:51.749
Safe error handling prevents database details from reaching users.

21
00:01:51.749 --> 00:02:01.546
Logging and monitoring help operators see unusual validation failures, repeated database errors, or administrative activity that deserves review.
